<div class="wrap">
<header>
  <p class="eyebrow">JusPrin &middot; design system audit</p>
  <h1>Agent Panel Token Sweep</h1>
  <p class="standfirst">The Agent panel drew its corners and text at sizes the design system does not define. Here is every value that was off, what it became, and the three places the brief describing the work was itself wrong.</p>
  <div class="meta">
    <div><b>Branch</b><span class="mono">claude/agent-panel-token-sweep-943665</span></div>
    <div><b>Commits</b><span class="mono">4fce662163 &middot; cd353772cb</span></div>
    <div><b>Base</b><span class="mono">jusprin-newui @ 8d6f29020e</span></div>
    <div><b>Date</b><span>7 September 2026</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<dl class="strip">
  <div><dt>Radii corrected</dt><dd>15</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Type sizes corrected</dt><dd>16<small> +1 split</small></dd></div>
  <div><dt>Off-scale values left</dt><dd>0<small> &nbsp;of type or radius</small></dd></div>
  <div><dt>Tests</dt><dd>65<small> &nbsp;pass, unchanged</small></dd></div>
  <div><dt>Net height, chat</dt><dd>&minus;2<small> px</small></dd></div>
</dl>

<div class="col">
<h2>What was wrong</h2>
<p class="lede">The panel resolves <em>colour</em> through the semantic tokens correctly. What had drifted was size and radius: values that exist nowhere in the token file, so the same kind of element was drawn differently in the panel than in the native header beside it.</p>
<p>The design system allows three radii &mdash; 6, 8 and 12 DIP &mdash; and five type roles: page title 24/30 bold, section 18/24 bold, body and control 14/20 regular, label 12/16 bold, dense metadata 10/14 regular. The panel used 4, 10 and 999 for radius, and 11, 13 and 16 for type. None of those is in the system.</p>
<p>This was not introduced by any recent change. It is the accumulated residue of a stylesheet written by hand against a token file it never reads.</p>
</div>

<h2>Discrepancies in the brief</h2>
<div class="col">
<p class="lede">The work was scoped by a written brief. Three of its conclusions did not survive contact with the code, and each changed what the fix had to be.</p>
</div>

<div class="col">
<div class="finding">
  <h3>The radius table omitted its largest group<span class="tag">scope&nbsp;&times;4</span></h3>
  <p>The brief listed three off-scale radii: <code>6px</code> in five places, <code>10px</code> in one, <code>999px</code> in three. It did not list <code>4px</code>, which appears eleven times &mdash; more than the other three combined.</p>
  <p>The omission has a clean explanation. The measurement was taken while the standard radius was still 4, so <code>4px</code> was on the scale at the moment it was counted. The standard then moved to 6, and the brief noted the move and flipped its verdict on the <code>6px</code> rows, calling them &ldquo;correct by accident.&rdquo; It never re-derived the <code>4px</code> rows that the same flip turns from correct into wrong.</p>
  <p>Those eleven included the base <code>button</code> rule and the composer textarea &mdash; precisely the standard controls the token change was about. The radius commit was roughly four times the size the brief implied.</p>
  <div class="evidence">$ git show --stat 8d6f29020e | grep styles.css
(no output &mdash; the commit that moved 4&nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp;6 never touched the stylesheet)</div>
</div>

<div class="finding">
  <h3>The menu-row exception did not apply<span class="tag">wrong&nbsp;fix</span></h3>
  <p>The brief stated that menu rows are &ldquo;a documented exception at 0: they are full-width inside a rounded popover.&rdquo; Two things were off.</p>
  <p>First, it was not documented. The rule lived only in two C++ comments in <code>Shell/HeaderControls.cpp</code>, at lines 319 and 508. Nothing in <code>design-system.md</code> mentioned it.</p>
  <p>Second, the condition does not hold here. The panel&rsquo;s <code>.chat-menu</code> sets <code>padding: 4px</code>, so its rows are inset by four pixels and are not full-width. Applied literally, the exception produced a visible defect: the focused row&rsquo;s square corner cut across the popover&rsquo;s own 8px radius. The rows took the standard 6 instead, and the real rule &mdash; which applies to the native header menu &mdash; is now written into <code>design-system.md</code> so the next person does not have to read C++ to find it.</p>
</div>

<div class="finding">
  <h3>Two roles were counted as one decision<span class="tag">shared&nbsp;rule</span></h3>
  <p>The brief counted <code>11px</code> four times. One of those four declarations covers two selectors:</p>
  <div class="evidence">.history-provenance,
.history-warnings { font-size: 11px; line-height: 16px; }</div>
  <p>They are not the same role. The provenance line is metadata &mdash; slicer version, printer, material. A slice warning is the most actionable line on a build card, and it sits directly above the control that writes to the user&rsquo;s filesystem. Treating them as one decision would have shrunk the warning to 10px along with the metadata around it.</p>
  <p>They were split: provenance takes dense metadata 10/14, the warning takes label 12/16 bold. It is now unmistakably louder than the metadata it sits beside, which is the hierarchy the card always should have had.</p>
</div>
</div>

