</div>

<h2>Density</h2>
<div class="col">
<p class="lede">Growing ten declarations from 13 to 14 was the flagged risk of this work. It largely cancelled itself out.</p>
<p>The chat screen finished <strong>two pixels shorter</strong> than it started: the ten sizes that grew are offset by the four that shrank. Nothing wraps that did not wrap before, and no control changed its footprint enough to reflow a row.</p>
<p>One screen does grow. The MCP settings review &mdash; the screen where the user reads what JusPrin is about to write into their configuration file &mdash; is <strong>48 pixels taller</strong>, and the long helper path now wraps onto a third line. That is the correct trade: the diff is the one thing on screen the reader must actually read, and body size is the role for it.</p>
</div>

<figure>
  <img alt="Side-by-side of the MCP settings review screen. The right version has slightly larger text throughout and the command path wraps onto an additional line, pushing the Connect button lower." src="''' + imgs['diff'] + r'''">
  <figcaption><b>The one visible cost.</b> Left before, right after. The Connect button moves from y&nbsp;534 to y&nbsp;582 in the captured frame &mdash; 48px of growth, measured, not estimated.</figcaption>
</figure>

<div class="note">
  <span class="ui">Correction</span>
  <p>The commit message on <code class="mono">cd353772cb</code> states this screen grows &ldquo;about 74px.&rdquo; That figure was estimated from the screenshot before it was measured. The measured value is 48px. The commit message overstates it and has not been amended.</p>
</div>

<h2>Why it drifted</h2>
<div class="col">
<p class="lede">Both commits fix values. Neither fixes the mechanism that let the values go wrong, which is worth stating plainly because it will happen again.</p>
<p>The page has a token bridge: <code>src/.../AgentUI/src/tokens.ts</code> imports the token file and applies it as CSS custom properties. But it applies only the <code>semantic</code> group &mdash; the colours. The <code>dimension</code> group, which holds the spacing scale, the radius scale and the type roles, is never applied at all. Every size and radius in the stylesheet is a hand-copied literal.</p>
<p>So when <code>dimension.radius.standard</code> moved from 4 to 6, the panel&rsquo;s eleven 4px radii silently became wrong and nothing detected it. Colours cannot drift this way; sizes always can.</p>
<p>Worth knowing alongside that: no native code reads the radius tokens either. Neither <code>ShellTheme.cpp</code> nor <code>tests/brand/test_brand_tokens.cpp</code> touches <code>dimension.radius</code>, which is why adding <code>pill</code> to the token file was safe &mdash; and also why that file is, for radius, documentation rather than a source of truth.</p>
<p>A follow-up task has been queued to emit the dimension group as CSS variables and replace the literals, with a test that fails when a raw px radius reappears.</p>
</div>

<h2>How it was verified</h2>
<div class="col">
<ul>
  <li>The existing suite &mdash; 65 tests &mdash; passes on both commits. It asserts behaviour, not pixels, so it proves the panel still works, not that it looks right.</li>
  <li>Looking at it was done with a throwaway harness: a mock host answering the bridge handshake, the real page mounted in Vite, driven through the chat, chat list, setup chooser, tool list, settings diff and progress screens. Before and after captured on each.</li>
  <li>The bundle at <code>resources/jusprin/agent/index.html</code> rebuilds byte-identically, so the committed artefact matches a clean build.</li>
  <li>The harness was removed before committing and is not in the repository. It is kept outside the tree and can be contributed as a dev tool on request.</li>
</ul>
</div>

